# How each grammar rule transforms the types carried through a parse.
#
# fragment.ebnf is a plain grammar: it says what text is well formed and nothing
# about types. This file says what the types do, one line per rule, and it is the
# only place that knows. Nothing here depends on a parser, so it can be read and
# tested on its own -- see maskgen/cpp/test_type_table.cc.
#
# Two values travel with the parser. REQUIRED is the type this position must end up
# producing, which comes from above: `let x : string =` requires a string of
# everything after it. PRODUCED is the type of what has been written here so far,
# which comes from below: after `count` it is number, after `count.toFixed` string.
#
# Each line is:   <rule>  <key>=<value> ...
# Every rule named here is underscored in fragment.ebnf, and every underscored rule
# there has a line here. The underscore means only "named from outside the grammar".
#
# enter=, applied when the parser starts a new occurrence of the rule:
#
#   inherit        take REQUIRED and PRODUCED from the enclosing position (default)
#   fresh          take REQUIRED, but start with nothing PRODUCED. For a position
#                  that begins a new value, such as a call argument
#   require_none   drop REQUIRED: this position may produce anything. For the
#                  operands of ==, which are constrained by each other rather than
#                  by whatever the comparison itself has to satisfy
#
# finish=, applied when the rule completes and the enclosing position moves past it:
#
#   pass           leave the enclosing PRODUCED alone
#   keep_first     adopt this rule's PRODUCED, unless something was produced already
#                  (default: right for an ordinary rule with one value-producing child)
#   replace        adopt this rule's PRODUCED, overwriting. For a continuation,
#                  where each step retypes everything before it: `msg` produced
#                  string, then `.length` makes the whole thing a number
#   produce <tag>  produce a type the environment names, whatever the text. <tag> is
#                  a name the environment resolves; it is never a type in this file
#   produce_text   produce the type the environment gives for the text matched,
#                  which is how a name gets the type it was declared with
#   require_text   set REQUIRED from the text matched, which is how a `: string`
#                  annotation constrains what follows it
#   combine <op>   ask the environment to combine what the enclosing position has
#                  PRODUCED with what this rule produced, under the named operator.
#                  The only action that looks at two types at once, and the only way
#                  to type an operator whose result depends on both operands
#   check          refuse to finish unless PRODUCED satisfies REQUIRED. Having
#                  passed, it carries PRODUCED out like keep_first: a check is a
#                  refusal, not a transformation, and `( count )` has to be able to
#                  tell the expression around it that it is a number
#
# content=lexicon  the legal text here is not in the grammar: it comes from the
#                  symbol table, and a byte is refused as soon as no name in scope
#                  still matches what has been written
#
# gate=environment the environment is asked before the parser may enter this rule at
#                  all, which is how a call on something that is not a function is
#                  refused without writing that into the grammar

# ---- where a requirement enters the parse ----
_type_ann       finish=require_text
_assign_target  finish=require_text  content=lexicon

# ---- where a requirement is discharged ----
_expr           enter=fresh          finish=check

# ---- operators ----
# A comparison is a boolean however its operands are typed, and its operands are
# unconstrained by what the comparison itself must satisfy.
_cmp            finish=produce boolean
_cmp_operand    enter=require_none   finish=pass
# `+` is typed from both operands, which is the only way to get TypeScript's rule
# right: the result is a number only when both are numbers, and a string as soon as
# either is one. Each operand combines into what precedes it, and the sum as a whole
# combines into what encloses it, so `3 + "x" + 4` folds left exactly as it reads.
_primary        finish=combine plus
_sum            finish=combine plus

# ---- literals ----
_num_lit        finish=produce number
_str_lit        finish=produce string
_bool_lit       finish=produce boolean

# ---- names ----
_name           finish=produce_text  content=lexicon
_member_name    finish=produce_text  content=lexicon

# ---- continuations: each step retypes the whole expression ----
_postfix        finish=replace
_trailer        finish=replace
_member_step    finish=replace       gate=environment
_call_step      finish=replace       gate=environment
